{
  "challenges": [
    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_sqlf_331",
      "name": "sanity-checks-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Which check catches it?",
      "description": "Run the lesson's sanity checks in your head on a broken report, then decide what really happens when a value is pasted into SQL with an f-string.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "A second analyst \"fixes\" the incident report by dividing each country by the report's own total instead of finance's total. The new report for Q1 2026 is:\n\nVN    67000 cents   47.9 %\nDE    30000 cents   21.4 %\nUS    25000 cents   17.9 %\nvn    18000 cents   12.9 %   (the country is 'vn ')\n\nThe independent total of paid Q1 orders is 125000 cents, and the countries with paid Q1 orders are VN, DE and US (after trimming and upper-casing). Which checks from this lesson FAIL? (Select all that apply.)",
          "explanation": "The parts add up to 67000 + 30000 + 25000 + 18000 = 140000 cents, not 125000, so the reconcile check fails: the join still fans out. 'VN' and 'vn ' have the same key once spaces are trimmed and the text is upper-cased, so the grain check fails too. The percentages now add up to 100.1, which is within 0.5 of 100, and every one of them is between 0 and 100, so both range checks pass, and every expected country has a row. That is the lesson of this question: percentages computed from the report's own total always add up to about 100, even when every number is wrong. Only a total computed by a second, independent query catches the fan-out.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "Reconcile: the parts do not add up to the independent total.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Grain: two rows have the same key.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Percent sum: the percentages are more than 0.5 away from 100.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Percent range: a percentage is below 0 or above 100.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Completeness: a country with paid orders has no row.",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The customers table has 5 rows, and no customer's country is 'x'. A report script reads a country from a web form, and a visitor types:\n\nx' OR 1=1 --\n\nWhat do these two lines return?\n\n(A) db.execute(f\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers WHERE country = '{country}'\").fetchone()\n(B) db.execute(\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers WHERE country = ?\", (country,)).fetchone()",
          "explanation": "In (A) the text is pasted into the SQL before SQLite reads it, so the database receives WHERE country = 'x' OR 1=1 --'. The quote closes the string, OR 1=1 is true for every row, and -- turns the leftover quote into a comment: the query counts all 5 customers. That is SQL injection: input that changes the meaning of the query. In (B) the SQL text never changes; the driver sends the value separately and SQLite compares it as one string, so it looks for a customer whose country is literally \"x' OR 1=1 --\" and finds none: (0,). Placeholders work for values only; table and column names cannot be parameters.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "(A) returns (5,), (B) returns (0,).",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Both return (0,): no country is equal to that text.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "(A) raises a syntax error, (B) returns (0,).",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Both return (5,): the ? placeholder pastes the text in the same way.",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
